Fixed in f4448ec, pending CI green.

You are right, and I checked the semantics rather than assuming: Ginkgo's `DeferCleanup` contract is that if the last return value is a non-nil error it fails the spec, so the logging version really could have swallowed a failed cleanup.

```go
func deletePodImmediately(ctx context.Context, podClient *e2epod.PodClient, podName string) error {
	err := podClient.Delete(ctx, podName, *metav1.NewDeleteOptions(0))
	if apierrors.IsNotFound(err) {
		return nil
	}
	return err
}
```

`NotFound` still ignored for idempotency, since each spec deletes the pod itself before cleanup runs. I added a line to the doc comment explaining why it returns rather than logs, so it does not get "simplified" back later.

Squashed into the original commit rather than stacked, to keep this a single-commit PR.

Agreed with reverting — removing the loop wasn't what I meant to suggest; multiple rounds over `bestRemainder` are by design, so keeping parity with `takeByTopologyNUMADistributed` makes sense. And fair point on (b) under the assumption that `cpuGroupSize` stays stable across allocations. My original ask was only a cheap escape hatch, e.g.:

```go
for remainder > 0 {
    progressed := false
    for _, numa := range bestRemainder {
        ...
        remainder -= cpuGroupSize
        progressed = true
    }
    if !progressed {
        return cpuset.New(), fmt.Errorf("accounting error, cannot place remainder: %d", remainder)
    }
}
```

Given how easily the invariants shift here (https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/140629#discussion_r3615342530 — the fail-early commit quietly broke the internal-call contract and only the UTs caught it), a deterministic bail-out feels like cheap insurance. Totally fine as a follow-up though.
